# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AutoRnD configuration
#
# Copy to .env and fill in the REQUIRED sections. AutoRnD will not start
# until all four required model tiers name a model. The optional tiers can
# stay empty — each simply turns its feature off.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# REQUIRED — provider access
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Your API key. The client speaks the OpenAI chat-completions protocol,
# so any compatible provider works.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# Point this at whichever provider you use. Must be the /v1 root — AutoRnD
# appends /chat/completions and /models to it.
OPENROUTER_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# REQUIRED — model tiers
#
# These six run on every workflow. AutoRnD will not start without them.
#
# AutoRnD routes each phase to a tier, and you decide what fills it. There
# are no defaults and no recommendations: model quality, pricing and
# availability change faster than this file can, and the right pick depends
# on your provider, budget and domain.
#
# Format is provider/model exactly as your provider lists it — the value
# must contain a "/". On startup AutoRnD checks every id against your
# provider's catalogue and reports unknown ones in /api/health.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# TRIAGE — one call per workflow, at the very start.
# Classifies domain and risk, and picks which specialists participate.
# Short prompt, short structured output, no reasoning needed.
# Optimise for: price and reliable JSON. This is the cheapest tier.
MODEL_TRIAGE=

# ENGINEERING — the workhorse. Runs implement, validate, review and plan
# feasibility, so it is called more than every other tier combined and
# dominates your bill.
# Optimise for: the best capability you can afford at volume. If you tune
# one value in this file, tune this one.
MODEL_ENGINEERING=

# ARCHITECTURE — planning, and review of critical-risk work.
# Called once or twice per workflow, but its plan constrains everything
# downstream: a weak plan wastes every token spent after it.
# Optimise for: reasoning quality over price. Cheap here is false economy.
MODEL_ARCHITECTURE=

# ESCALATION — failure autopsy, only after the implement/validate loop has
# exhausted MAX_ITERATIONS. Reads the full failure log and issues a recovery
# directive, or stops the run for human intervention.
# Rarely called, so an expensive model here costs little in practice.
# Optimise for: reasoning depth on long, messy input.
MODEL_ESCALATION=

# RESEARCH — briefs the other phases before any work starts. Useful whether or
# not you have documentation:
#
#   With docs ingested  — writes the search queries, reads the ranked results,
#                         and briefs on the constraints, interfaces and values
#                         this work must respect, naming what the docs do not
#                         cover. Two small calls per workflow.
#   With no docs        — scopes the request instead: restates the objective,
#                         lists what the request leaves unspecified, and states
#                         the assumptions being made so they can be challenged
#                         rather than buried in a plan. One small call.
#
# It is told never to assert a project fact it was not given; unknowns are
# reported, not filled in. Output is labelled as analysis, not documentation.
# Optimise for: faithful summarisation. It must not invent facts.
# Unset: phases run on the raw request alone.
MODEL_RESEARCH=

# SEARCH — looks outward for facts the project documentation does not cover.
# Needs a search-capable model: a ":online" variant of any model, or one with
# native search built in.
#
# This is the anti-confabulation tier, and it exists because of a measurement.
# Asked which charger IC a specific board uses, a capable model answered with a
# part that does not do that job — confidently, in bold, with no hedge. Asked
# the EU 868 MHz limit, it gave "+14 dBm EIRP", conflating ERP with EIRP, two
# units 2.15 dB apart: the difference between a compliant radio and a failed
# certification. A search-backed model got both right and cited its sources.
#
# Both wrong answers sounded exactly as certain as the right ones, so there is
# no confidence signal to gate a lookup on. At roughly half a cent per lookup,
# priced against one wrong component in a bill of materials, it is not close.
#
# Only gaps are searched — facts the briefing already established are missing —
# and findings are ingested, so a fact is looked up once and local thereafter.
# Unsourced answers are shown to you but never stored as project knowledge.
#
# What to look for when choosing one, from comparing several on real datasheet
# questions. The cheapest search model is rarely the wrong call, but the
# differences that showed up were worth more than the price gap:
#
#   - Names the document, not the subject. "LoRaWAN Regional Parameters
#     RP002-1.0.3" is something you can put in a compliance file; "the LoRaWAN
#     spec" is not.
#   - Says what kind of number it found. 500 mA as a *programmable regulation*
#     value is a different fact from 500 mA as an absolute maximum rating, and
#     designing against the wrong one damages hardware.
#   - Admits when a figure is not universal. Asked for LiFePO4 cutoff voltages,
#     the better answer was "these vary by cell, here are the datasheets"
#     rather than one confident number.
#
# Note also that search models are priced per request as much as per token, so
# a headline rate several times higher often works out near parity on the short
# question, short answer shape a lookup actually uses. Measure rather than
# assume: one pairing listed at 15x on completion measured 1.98x in practice.
MODEL_SEARCH=


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# OPTIONAL — ranking
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# RANKER — orders retrieved documentation by usefulness before it enters a
# prompt. Retrieval finds material about the right subject; ranking decides
# which of it actually helps.
# A purpose-built rerank model is used automatically when your provider serves
# one — it scores rather than generates, so it is cheaper and sharper than a
# chat model. Any chat model also works, via listwise ranking.
# Note: rerank models often do not appear in a provider's chat-model listing.
# AutoRnD confirms them separately, so a valid one is not reported missing.
# Idle until you ingest documentation — there is nothing to rank before then.
# Unset: falls back to MODEL_RESEARCH, then to raw retrieval order.
MODEL_RANKER=


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# OPTIONAL — premium tier
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# PREMIUM — powers the user-initiated "Double Check" review, an independent
# second opinion on a finished workflow. It re-reads the entire workflow, so
# cost scales with workflow size; the estimate endpoint shows the projected
# spend before you commit.
# Leave empty to disable the feature — the button will not appear.
MODEL_PREMIUM=


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Engine
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Implement/validate attempts before the escalation autopsy triggers. (1-20)
# Every extra iteration is another paid round trip — raise deliberately.
MAX_ITERATIONS=5

# Ceiling on one outward lookup. A lookup wants figures and their sources, not
# an essay. Note that a reasoning model will spend this budget thinking before
# it emits anything, so pair a low value with a non-reasoning search model.
#
# Search is the most expensive tier by a wide margin — measured at 61% of a full
# workflow and 98% of a grounding sweep. It is bounded two ways: at most three
# lookups per workflow, and the knowledge store is consulted before any of them,
# since a fact looked up once is local afterwards.
SEARCH_MAX_TOKENS=1200

# Ceiling on the validate phase, which judges work rather than redoing it. An
# unbounded validator once produced 15k output tokens for a green/red verdict.
# Do not set this near 3000: the default engineering tier is often a reasoning
# model, and at 3000 it spent the whole budget thinking and emitted nothing
# three times over, failing the run having paid for all of it. A ceiling is
# billed only when used, so headroom is free on the runs that were fine.
VALIDATE_MAX_TOKENS=8000

# Hard ceiling on the escalation model's response, guarding against a reasoning
# model running away on a long failure log.
# Do not set this too low. Reasoning models spend this budget thinking before
# they emit anything, so a tight cap returns an empty reply with
# finish_reason=length rather than a short answer — the autopsy then fails on
# exactly the runs that needed it.
ESCALATION_MAX_TOKENS=16384

# Fresh attempts granted after an escalation directive.
ESCALATION_RECOVERY_ATTEMPTS=3


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Server
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Bind address. Defaults to loopback: AutoRnD has no rate limiting and
# spends real money, so do not expose it directly. For remote access put it
# behind a reverse proxy with TLS, or on a private network.
# Containers need 0.0.0.0 to accept forwarded traffic — the bundled
# Dockerfile already passes that on the command line.
# Read only when launching via `python -m autornd.main`; a `uvicorn ...`
# command line takes its own --host/--port.
API_HOST=127.0.0.1
API_PORT=8100

# DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR, CRITICAL
LOG_LEVEL=INFO


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Storage
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Workflow state and history. Must use an async driver.
DATABASE_URL=sqlite+aiosqlite:///./autornd.db

# Vector store for ingested project documentation.
CHROMADB_PATH=./chromadb_data


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Authentication
#
# All three settings are optional. With none of them set, every endpoint is
# open and unauthenticated callers can see all workflows and spend your
# credits — fine on a trusted network, not otherwise.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Static bearer token for programmatic access:
#   curl -H "Authorization: Bearer <key>" http://localhost:8100/api/workflows
API_KEY=

# Signing secret for user accounts. Generated at startup if empty, which
# invalidates every session on restart — set it to keep people logged in.
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
JWT_SECRET=

# Set false once your accounts exist, so the register endpoint stops
# accepting new ones.
REGISTRATION_ENABLED=true


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Project grounding
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Which workflow graph to run, from workflows/<name>.yaml. The shape of a
# workflow — which phases run, in what order, what gates them — lives in that
# file rather than in the code, so you can copy one and change it.
#   engineering-rnd  the full team: feasibility, domain review, escalation
#   lean             plan, build, verify. No review team, no escalation.
AUTORND_WORKFLOW=engineering-rnd

# Loads profiles/<name>.yaml, which supplies your stack, constraints and
# per-specialist context. Leave empty for the generic profile.
#
# Note: the documentation directory is derived from the profile's `name:`
# field, not this filename — a profile named "MyProject" reads docs from
# docs/myproject/. A missing directory is skipped silently, so check
# `python -m autornd.cli stats` if your docs do not appear to load.
#
# A profile can also declare the vocabularies your team actually uses:
#   domains:  domain name -> lead specialist, or {lead, checks} to add the
#             validation questions that domain is judged by
#   roles:    role name -> {name, domain, tier, expertise} for a role this
#             harness does not ship (a paralegal, a copywriter)
# Triage is offered the shipped defaults plus yours and may name something in
# neither. An unmapped domain falls back to the systems architect and an
# undeclared role becomes a generalist, rather than failing. See the README.
# Pin which upstream serves your requests, comma separated, highest priority
# first — for example "OpenInference,StreamLake". Empty lets the provider
# choose, which favours availability.
#
# These are not interchangeable goals. Measured across two runs of the same
# 36-sector eval suite with no code change between them, one model id moved
# provider and the results moved with it: replies dropped to 70 completion
# tokens, the price fell roughly thirtyfold, and four sectors classified risk
# lower than before — two of them safety-critical. A pin disables fallbacks, so
# a run either uses the provider you named or fails loudly.
OPENROUTER_PROVIDER_ORDER=

AUTORND_PROFILE=
